\chapter{Use of the System from the Terminal}
\label{chap:usage}

This chapter describes how the terminal version of the system is started and
what its commands do; the bot and the web API, which drive the same engine, are
described in chapter~\ref{chap:interfaces}.


\section{Starting the System}

The system requires SWI-Prolog. It is started from the root of the repository
by consulting the bootstrap file:
\begin{lstlisting}
$ swipl prolog/start.pl
\end{lstlisting}
At start-up the user chooses the language of the session and then the
uncertainty method (cf. section~\ref{sec:uncertainty}). Changing the method requires a restart, so that the certainties of a consultation are all
computed in the same way.
\begin{lstlisting}
Select language / Seleziona lingua:
	1: English
	2: Italiano
 > 1.

Select the uncertainty method:
	1: Certainty factors (minimum t-norm)
	2: Fuzzy logic (product t-norm)
	3: Fuzzy logic with membership functions (graded duration/frequency)
	4: Fuzzy logic (Lukasiewicz t-norm)
	5: Fuzzy logic (Hamacher product t-norm)
	6: Fuzzy logic (Einstein product t-norm)
 > 1.
\end{lstlisting}
A short presentation and the list of the commands follow, and the shell waits
for a command. An input that is not a command (or, later, not a valid answer to
a question) is rejected with an error message and read again.
\begin{lstlisting}
-------------------------------------------------------------------
-- PESAD - Prolog Expert System for Anxiety,
--         Obsessive-Compulsive and Trauma-Related Disorders
--
--	knowledge base: DSM-5-TR
--	author:         Donato Meoli
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Available commands:
	investigation.	- Run the full investigation of all disorders;
	control.	- Run the check of a single disorder;
	facts.		- Show the asserted facts;
	help.		- Show the list of available commands;
	clean.		- Clear the memory;
	quit.		- Close the interface.
\end{lstlisting}


\section{Commands}

The six commands are \code{investigation}, which investigates all the
diagnostic goals; \code{control}, which checks a single goal chosen by the user;
\code{facts}, which prints the facts stated in the current consultation;
\code{help}, which prints the list of the commands; \code{clean}, which clears the
working memory; and \code{quit}, which closes the shell. Questions, diagnoses
and explanations are printed in the chosen language. During a consultation the
engine poses a question whenever a fact it needs is not in the working memory.
A symptom question is answered \code{yes} or \code{no} (or \code{why}, cf.
section~\ref{sec:acquisition}), and a certainty in $[0,1]$ is then requested
for the answer.

\subsection{Investigation}

The command \code{investigation} poses the question on the age band and then
tries to prove the goals of table~\ref{tab:disorders}, posing the questions
that each one needs and that have not been answered yet. Its result is the list
of the goals with a certainty greater than $0$, sorted by decreasing certainty.
Two choices characterize it. First, the exploration does not stop at the first
goal that succeeds, since comorbidity is common among these disorders (e.g.,
panic disorder and agoraphobia, or ADHD and autism spectrum disorder) and the
manual allows several of them to be assigned together. Second, the goals
examined are reported with their certainty, including the weak ones. A
clinician who disagrees with the ranking can thus open the proof tree of any
goal and find the premise responsible for the difference. In the code,
\begin{lstlisting}
solve(investigation) :-
    delete_trees,
    investigate_goals(Goals_List),
    descending_order_goals(Goals_List, Sorted_Goals_List),
    display_investigation(Sorted_Goals_List),
    investigation_how(Sorted_Goals_List).
\end{lstlisting}
where \code{delete\_trees/0} removes the proof trees of the previous runs, but
not the facts stated by the user, which remain valid across runs of the same
consultation (the two are kept apart so that a run interrupted by an error does
not leave partial trees in memory). Then \code{investigate\_goals/1}
collects the goals that can be proved, with their certainty, and
\code{descending\_order\_goals/2} and \code{display\_investigation/1} sort and
print them. Finally, \code{investigation\_how/1} offers the proof tree of one
goal at a time, chosen by its number in the list, until the user declines. An
abridged session follows (in a real one the questions are more numerous, and
the certainties less round):
\begin{lstlisting}
> investigation.

What is the patient's age group?
	1: <= 6 years
	2: 7-12 years
	3: 13-17 years
	4: >= 18 years
 > 4.

[ ... ]

Has an abrupt surge of intense fear or intense discomfort that reaches
a peak within minutes
? (yes, no) > yes.
Enter the certainty degree (from 0.0 to 1.0) > 0.9.

[ ... ]

========================== DIAGNOSIS ==========================

1) Panic Disorder with a certainty degree of 72%

2) Agoraphobia with a certainty degree of 64%

Do you want to know how they were deduced? (yes, no) > yes.

Enter the code corresponding to the chosen option > 1.

[ ... the proof tree of Panic Disorder ... ]

Do you want to know how they were deduced? (yes, no) > no.
\end{lstlisting}
A complete investigation poses several hundred questions, since the knowledge
base has 238 yes/no items. This is the price of ranking all the goals (cf.
section~\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Control}

The command \code{control} checks a single goal and poses only the questions of
that goal. It is useful when the clinician has already formed a hypothesis and
wants to confirm or reject it:
\begin{lstlisting}
solve(control) :-
    selection_goal(Goal_Name),
    delete_tree(Goal_Name),
    solve_goal(Goal_Name, CF),
    display_control(Goal_Name, CF),
    control_how(Goal_Name).
\end{lstlisting}
Here \code{selection\_goal/1} lets the user choose the goal from the list of the
goals of the knowledge base, and \code{delete\_tree/1} removes its previous
proof tree. Then \code{solve\_goal/2} proves it (after the question on the age
band, as in the investigation), \code{display\_control/2} prints its certainty
and \code{control\_how/1} offers its proof tree.

\subsection{Facts, Help, Clean and Quit}

The command \code{facts} prints the facts stated in the consultation, each as
\code{(Answer, Attribute, Value, Certainty)}. Here \code{Answer} is \code{yes} or
\code{no} (always \code{yes} for a multiple-choice question, whose fact is the
option chosen), \code{Attribute} is the kind of question (\code{symptom}, or the
name of a multiple-choice question), \code{Value} is the item or the option, and
\code{Certainty} is the certainty given with the answer. For instance,
\begin{lstlisting}
> facts.

============================ FACTS ============================
(yes,patient,adult,1.0)
(yes,symptom,palpitations,0.9)
(no,symptom,panic_better_explained_by_other_disorder,1.0)
(yes,symptoms_duration,three_to_six_months,1.0)
\end{lstlisting}
The command \code{help} prints the list of the commands again. The command
\code{clean} removes both the proof trees and the facts, and it is used to start
the consultation of a new patient. Finally, \code{quit} clears the working
memory in the same way and closes the shell.
